    "description": "Iran’s waters include the enclosed Caspian Sea, the ocean-connected Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman, and inland lakes sustained by their catchments. Their different drainage settings explain why water levels, salinity, shorelines, and environmental pressures must be interpreted separately.",
    "tags": ["Seas", "Lakes", "Drainage basins", "Caspian Sea", "Persian Gulf", "Water balance"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:55:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:55:41Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Seas and Lakes: Connected Coasts and Inland Basins | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the differences between the Caspian Sea, Iran’s southern marine waters, and closed-basin lakes such as Lake Urmia."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-water-worlds",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Begin with the outlet",
        "content": "The first question to ask about an Iranian sea or lake is where its water can go. The Caspian has no natural outlet to the ocean. The Persian Gulf, by contrast, connects through the Strait of Hormuz to the Gulf of Oman and onward to the Arabian Sea. Lake Urmia is another enclosed system, receiving water from rainfall and rivers and losing it through evaporation. These distinctions are more useful than the names 'sea' and 'lake' alone. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nA closed drainage basin is often called endorheic: its water does not flow out to the world ocean. Iranica distinguishes the Caspian, Lake Urmia, Persian Gulf, and interior drainage regions in its geographical account of Iran. These are drainage settings rather than interchangeable types of shoreline. They connect the study of seas and lakes to mountains, rivers, and water use farther inland. [s1]\n\nFor readers exploring Iran, this provides a practical organizing principle. Begin with the basin, then ask how the water body receives freshwater, what removes water, and how its shoreline responds. A single national narrative of 'drying lakes' cannot adequately describe every marine coast and inland depression. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "southern-connection",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The southern waters form a connected route",
        "content": "The Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman are distinct but connected waters. Between them lies the Strait of Hormuz, located between Iran and Oman. The US Energy Information Administration describes this passage as a major energy-transport chokepoint: a narrow section of a much wider shipping network through which substantial oil and gas traffic passes. Its geographical importance comes from this position between the Gulf and the open sea. [s2]\n\nThat connection helps explain why the two southern waters belong together in an overview while retaining separate pages. The Persian Gulf is the inner body reached through the strait; the Gulf of Oman provides the onward marine connection. A map should show this sequence clearly rather than implying that either is an enclosed inland lake. This page describes the physical route and does not assess current navigation conditions or shipping restrictions. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "closed-basin-examples",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Two enclosed waters, different scales",
        "content": "The Caspian’s environmental pressures extend across an international catchment. UNEP identifies river discharge, evaporation, precipitation, winds, human water use, and diversions as interacting influences on its changing level. Its call for coordinated research reflects a central difficulty: identifying the relative contribution of these processes requires observations across the basin, beyond any single country’s coast. [s4]\n\nLake Urmia offers a more concentrated example of closed-basin sensitivity. The US Geological Survey explains that diverted inflows can substantially alter the lake’s balance, because water reaching the lake has no outlet except evaporation. Its satellite account also distinguishes the shallower southern basin from the northern basin: retreat need not occur evenly around one lake. NASA’s historical account similarly identifies drought and agricultural withdrawals as pressures on this saline water body. Neither source should be read as a report of its precise condition in October 2026. [s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "compare-carefully",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a changing shoreline can establish",
        "content": "Satellite images can make water loss visible, but they do not make every comparison equivalent. The Lake Urmia material explains how infrared imagery distinguishes water from surrounding land and how shallow water can appear differently from deeper water. Comparing images therefore requires attention to acquisition dates and interpretation, rather than treating every colored boundary as an identical measurement. [s3]\n\nThe wider lesson is to separate a water body’s location, its hydrological behavior, and the date of an observation. This guide’s individual pages distinguish the Caspian Sea, Persian Gulf, Gulf of Oman, Lake Urmia, Hamun Lakes, and Namak Lake. The overview establishes their drainage context; site-specific accounts must supply the evidence for local conditions, ecological change, and any numerical trends. A photograph or historic shoreline cannot substitute for a dated assessment. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "southern-water-route",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The southern marine connection",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Persian Gulf\"] -->|\"Marine connection through\"| B[\"Strait of Hormuz\"]\n  B -->|\"Opens into\"| C[\"Gulf of Oman\"]\n  C -->|\"Onward connection to\"| D[\"Arabian Sea\"]\n  D -->|\"Part of\"| E[\"Indian Ocean\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows show geographical connectivity from the Persian Gulf toward the open ocean. They do not represent a one-way current, shipping authorization, or political control.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
