    "description": "The Caspian Sea is the world’s largest enclosed inland water body, shared by Iran, Azerbaijan, Russia, Kazakhstan, and Turkmenistan. Its declining water level links Iran’s northern coast to environmental changes and water-management decisions across a much larger international basin.",
    "tags": ["Caspian Sea", "Northern Iran", "Hydrology", "Coastal change", "Biodiversity", "Environmental cooperation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:55:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:55:41Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Caspian Sea: Iran’s Northern Shore and a Changing Water Balance | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand the Caspian’s enclosed basin, uneven shoreline retreat, ecological pressures, and international environmental cooperation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "shared-inland-sea",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An inland sea with an international catchment",
        "content": "The Caspian is the world’s largest inland water body. Iran shares its shoreline with Azerbaijan, Russia, Kazakhstan, and Turkmenistan, making the northern Iranian coast one part of a much larger environmental system. A research explanation published by the United Nations University emphasizes that changes in rivers across this shared basin affect the sea’s condition. Water management far from an Iranian beach can therefore matter to that beach’s future. [s1]\n\nThe key to understanding the Caspian is its water balance. UNEP identifies precipitation, river discharge, evaporation, winds, human water use, and diversions as interacting influences. Its assessment calls for coordinated research rather than treating shoreline retreat as a phenomenon that can be explained from local rainfall alone. The Caspian is enclosed, so its behavior differs fundamentally from an ocean coast exposed to global sea-level rise. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "explaining-the-decline",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Climate and upstream water use",
        "content": "A June 2026 explanation by researchers Nima Shokri and Amir AghaKouchak argues that the recent decline reflects both warming-related evaporation and reduced river inflows associated with human activity. Their account particularly emphasizes the engineered Volga River system and the interception of water upstream. This is an attributed research interpretation, not a claim that every component of the Caspian’s water budget is settled. [s1]\n\nUNEP’s earlier assessment stresses the need to examine climate and water use together. Read alongside the newer research account, it supports a careful conclusion: management responses need to consider the entire basin, while the relative contributions of particular drivers depend on the period, observations, and analytical method. A single-cause explanation is inadequate for the evidence described here. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "unequal-shoreline-effects",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Retreat does not look the same everywhere",
        "content": "A peer-reviewed study published in Communications Earth & Environment examines the exceptionally shallow northeastern Caspian using satellite imagery. It documents retreat across habitats containing reed beds, shoals, channels, and sites used by fish, waterbirds, and Caspian seals. Falling water can disconnect animals from formerly accessible habitat even where the change appears on a map simply as newly exposed land. [s3]\n\nThese observations should not be transferred directly to Iran’s shoreline. They establish consequences in a particular northeastern study area, not an Iranian coastal retreat rate. Their wider value is to show why bathymetry—the shape and depth of the underwater surface—matters. The ecological and infrastructure effects of a change in water level depend on the local coast, rather than on the basin-wide level alone. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "environmental-cooperation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cooperation is a framework, not proof of recovery",
        "content": "The Tehran Convention provides a shared environmental framework for the Caspian states. Its stated purposes include protecting the sea from pollution and preserving its biological resources. The convention text also addresses coastal management, water-level fluctuations, environmental assessment, and monitoring. These provisions describe agreed responsibilities; their existence does not establish that ecological decline has been reversed. [s4][s5]\n\nFor understanding Iran’s Caspian coast, the most useful perspective joins local evidence to basin-wide processes. Shoreline observations can reveal where change is occurring, ecological studies can explain what is affected, and river and climate records can help investigate why. International agreements provide a structure for cooperation, while measured outcomes remain a separate question. [s2][s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "The Caspian Sea has lost an area nearly the size of Sicily: human activities are a major reason why",
        "publisher": "United Nations University, Institute for Water, Environment and Health",
